    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Determinants of the future is and always has been a powerful capital to possess and participate in. Storytelling as possibly one of the most determining tools of the future finds its exemplar self in the medium of film. The concrete form to storytelling’s abstraction is technology. Technology globally has determined the advancement of society, i.e. the future. STS which argues that technology is a fixed practice of society, which inadvertently subverts that belief. Scholars for a long time found science fiction, which heavily engaged in technological societies and futures, to be an artistic meditation of determining possible futures. More recently, due to the success of the film Black Panther, another artistic meditation of determining the future was popularized in the eyes of the global public: Afrofuturism. This paper explores how Black Panther artistically manifested Afrofuturism; how those manifestations navigated socio-technological problems within the world of the film; and how the film’s audience received those manifestations. What kind of Afrofuturism is being portrayed in the film, by the production of the film, and what kind of discussion resulted from that? Lastly, what kind of future was accepted, critiqued, and ultimately could arise?",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Johnston, Corazón. (2024). On Black Panther (2018) Afrofuturist Sociotechnical Imaginary Practices in Action. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/mf645sg7780. https://doi.org/10.25740/mf645sg7780.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
    ],
